<pdd-reason>The way out of the Vault. Everything else in this system moves a feature forward; this is the only path that moves one back into play, and without it the archive is a place ideas go to die quietly.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"router","signature":"router: APIRouter","returns":"POST /api/features/{feature_id}/reboot"},
  {"name":"reboot_feature","signature":"POST /api/features/{feature_id}/reboot","returns":"200 Feature | 401 | 404 | 422 not_archived"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/constants_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/orchestration/decisions_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `backend/routes/lifecycle.py`. It is a **separate router that shares the `/api/features`
prefix**, exactly as `votes.py` does — the reboot is a lifecycle transition rather than part of the
board read, and keeping it out of `features.py` leaves that module's read path untouched.

<responsibility>
Bring an archived request back into play with a clean voting window, so the Vault is a shelf rather than a grave.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT re-screen, re-dedup, or re-shape the request. It was screened when it was pitched and it is the same idea; sending it back through the pipeline would spend agent calls on work already done.
This module DOES NOT enqueue anything to Redis. The frozen spec says "no re-enqueue".
This module DOES NOT charge a Pitch Coin. Reviving an existing idea is not pitching a new one.
This module DOES NOT archive anything — the sprint's end-of-cycle maintenance owns the outbound direction (US-07).
This module DOES NOT read the board or serve any list; `features.py` owns every read.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
Reboot: moving one `ARCHIVED` feature back to `VOTING` with a fresh window. The frozen `openapi.yaml` defines it as "ARCHIVED -> VOTING; resets created_at and upvotes to 1; no re-enqueue".
Fresh window: a reset `created_at`. Both the "newest" sort and the sprint's decay sweep measure from it, so without the reset a revived request is instantly stale again and the next sweep re-archives it.
Reviver: the identified user who pressed the control. Their vote is the one the new window starts with; they need not be the original author.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY read and write `feature_requests` and `feature_votes` through the injected Supabase client.
- MAY file a decision.
- MUST NOT call a model, publish to Redis, or touch the intake path.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Implement `POST /api/features/{feature_id}/reboot` exactly as the frozen `openapi.yaml` declares it — `ARCHIVED` → `VOTING`, `created_at` and `upvotes` reset, no re-enqueue — returning the updated `Feature`.
R2 (MUST): Require an identified user and answer `401` without one, but MUST NOT require the original author. The point is present demand: if only the author could revive an idea, one whose author has moved on stays archived however many people now want it.
R3 (MUST): Answer `422` with `error.code` exactly `not_archived` when the feature exists but is in any other status. The control only ever appears on the Vault, so this means the client is looking at a stale board — it is a refusal, not a server failure.
R4 (MUST): Answer `404` for a feature id that does not exist, distinctly from `422`.
R5 (MUST): Set `upvotes` to **1**, not 0 — the reviver's own vote, exactly as a fresh pitch starts at 1 with its author's. Zero would put a feature on the board that nobody appears to want, including the person who just asked for it.
R6 (MUST): Write the reviver's `feature_votes` row in the same operation. A count of 1 with no vote row is the inconsistency that let an author double-vote their own pitch once already; `upvotes` and the number of vote rows must agree.
R7 (MUST): Delete the feature's prior `feature_votes` rows as part of the reset. The new window measures current demand — leaving the old rows would both contradict the count and stop everyone who voted last time from voting again.
R8 (MUST): Reset `created_at` to now. It is what the "newest" sort and the sprint's decay sweep both measure from; without it a revived request is immediately eligible for re-archiving and can round-trip to the Vault before anyone sees it.
R9 (MUST): Guard the whole transition on the row still being `ARCHIVED` at write time, so two people pressing reboot at once produce one reboot rather than two resets and two votes.
R10 (MUST NOT): Alter `title`, `description`, `author_id`, `author_handle`, `parent_id`, `extends_id`, `merge_count`, or `postpone_count`. It is the same idea getting another run; rewriting its identity or erasing its history would make the Vault a laundry.
R11 (MUST): Return the full `Feature` shape the board renders, with `children` present as a list, so the client can drop the row straight into the pipeline it now belongs to. **`author_id` MUST NOT appear in the response** — the features route already forbids it everywhere else, and reading a row that contains it is not a reason to echo it back.
R12 (MUST): File a decision at phase `lifecycle` with agent `reboot`, recording the feature and that a human triggered it, with `PROGRAMMATIC` as the model version — no model was involved. This is the one transition in the system a person causes directly, which makes it the one most worth being able to audit.
R13 (MUST NOT): Let a failed decision write change the response. The reboot happened whether or not the record could be filed.
R14 (MUST): Take the Supabase client and the caller's identity through the existing dependencies, never constructing either, so the whole path is testable against the fake the other routes already use.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
**A separate router rather than an addition to `features.py`.** `votes.py` already sets the
precedent of a second router sharing the `/api/features` prefix, and `features.py` is the largest,
most-patched module in the backend — its read path has been corrected several times and there is no
reason for a lifecycle write to put it at risk.

**Rate limiting is deliberately absent.** A reboot costs no coin and no agent call, and the only
thing it can do is move a feature the community had already stopped voting for back onto the board,
where it needs real votes to go anywhere. If reboot spam ever becomes a problem the honest fix is a
cooldown on the feature (one reboot per window), not a per-user limit — the point is present demand,
so who asked matters less than whether it keeps being asked.

**`postpone_count` survives on purpose.** A feature that was held twice before being archived carries
that history back with it; the architect should judge it in full knowledge of what happened last
time, not as though it were new.
</pdd>

